TI RAU-Net3D: a two-stage deep learning method for pneumothorax volume assessment with three-dimensional electrical impedance tomography
SO MEASUREMENT
DT Article
AB A two-stage deep learning method is proposed to quantitatively infer pleural gas and compressed lung regions from dynamic three-dimensional electrical impedance tomography (3D EIT) based on the RAU-Net3D network architecture. The RAU-Net3D model integrates 3D residual connections and 3D attention mechanisms within a 3D U-Net framework, effectively enhancing feature extraction and segmentation of pneumothorax regions. In the first stage, time-differential voltage data are pre-reconstructed into a coarse 3D ventilation pixel matrix using the 3D GREIT algorithm. In the second stage, the RAU-Net3D network establishes an end-to-end mapping from the 3D ventilation pixel matrix to the corresponding 3D CT pixel matrix. A total of 7380 training datasets were constructed using CT scans from 150 subjects with intact lungs and 60 patients diagnosed with pneumothorax. The proposed method was validated through a series of ablation experiments and human experiments, demonstrating significant advantages in pneumothorax localization and volume quantification. In the single-time-point measurements for three pneumothorax patients, the RAU-Net3D model achieved relative volume errors of 10.20 %, 11.31 %, and 8.36 % in pneumothorax detection compared to CT. During a dynamic monitoring experiment, the RAU-Net3D model achieved relative volume errors of 0.76 % and 4.48 % for the pre-drainage and postoperative stages compared to CT, respectively. These results demonstrate the potential of the model for noninvasive, radiation-free assessment of pneumothorax in dynamic bedside environments.
